        "content": "{\"cause_probable\": \"data_corruption\", \"resume\": \"L'alerte log_loss (25,23 vs référence 0,507, seuil 0,541) est expliquée par une corruption des données transmises au modèle : monthly_charges a été multiplié par 100 entre la source et les inputs reçus par le modèle (478 valeurs modifiées, ratio médian droit/gauche = 100, moyenne 71,91 -> 7191,06), toutes les autres colonnes étant inchangées. Le modèle servi n'a pas changé (reference_model et active_model portent le même hash de version), la population source ne montre aucun drift par rapport à la référence (tous les tests marginaux non significatifs, q~1), et le modèle prédit le churn pour 96,6 % des clients (483/500) avec des probabilités extrêmes (moyenne 0,968), ce qui produit le log_loss élevé et un ROC_AUC voisin de 0,50. Il s'agit donc d'une corruption des données d'entrée, ni d'un drift de population, ni d'une régression liée à une nouvelle version de modèle.\", \"preuves\": [\"E0007\",\"E0008\",\"E0009\",\"E0003\"]}",
        "additional_kwargs": {
          "reasoning_content": "Now I have a clear picture. Let me piece it together:\n\n1. compare_tables(source, inputs): monthly_charges changed for 478 values, ratio_count=478, median_right_over_left = 100.0, mean_right = 7191 vs mean_left = 71.91. So monthly_charges in inputs = source × 100 (×100 exactly). All other columns unchanged. So the data the model received has monthly_charges multiplied by 100! That's a data corruption in the pipeline feeding the model (a transformation/scale error).\n\n2. evaluate_predictions: confirms log_loss 25.23, accuracy 0.288, roc_auc 0.504, confusion matrix [[14,353],[3,130]] — model predicts churn for 96.6% (483/500). Because monthly_charges is ×100, the model (trained on normal charges ~72) sees charges of ~7200 and predicts near-certain churn for everyone.\n\n3. compare_distributions(source vs reference_clients): NO drift — all q_values ~1.0, p-values high, means nearly identical. So the source population is NOT drifted relative to reference. The drift is only in the inputs transformation (monthly_charges ×100), not in the underlying population.\n\n4. model_catalog: reference_model and active_model are the SAME version hash. So no model version change → not a deployment_regression via new model version. The model itself is unchanged; it's receiving corrupted inputs.\n\nSo the root cause: data_corruption — monthly_charges scaled by ×100 in the inputs received by the model (a pipeline/transformation error), causing the model to output extreme probabilities and huge log_loss. Not data drift (distributions match reference), not concept drift, not deployment regression (same model version).\n\nLet me make sure about \"data_corruption\" vs \"deployment_regression\". The corruption is in the inputs data (monthly_charges ×100). This is a data integrity issue upstream of the model → data_corruption. The model version is identical to reference, so it's not a model deployment regression. The predictions file shows model_version = same hash for all rows, confirming the same model served corrupted inputs.\n\nLet me also double check there's not something else. The probability min is 0.0285, max 1.0, mean 0.9676. With monthly_charges ×100, the model sees huge charges → predicts churn for almost all. This fits perfectly.\n\nShould I run compare_model_versions? The catalog says both models have identical version hash, so comparing them would yield identical results — not necessary and wouldn't add evidence. The evidence is sufficient.\n\nShould I run compare_conditional_relationship for concept drift? The source distribution matches reference (no drift), and the issue is clearly the ×100 scaling of monthly_charges in inputs. Concept drift would require the source (current) population's target/feature relationship to differ from reference — but there's no indication, and the corruption explains everything. I have enough.\n\nLet me also confirm: is there evidence this is deployment_regression? Deployment regression would be if the serving stack changed behavior. Here the model version is unchanged (same hash as reference), and the only difference is inputs monthly_charges ×100. So the root cause is data corruption in the input pipeline, not the model deployment itself. I'll classify as data_corruption.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether it could be deployment_regression. The inputs received by the model differ from source (monthly_charges ×100). This could be due to a faulty transformation in the deployment/inference pipeline. But the taxonomy distinguishes:\n- data_corruption: the data itself is corrupted\n- deployment_regression: the deployment changed and causes issues\n\nThe smoking gun is monthly_charges ×100 in inputs vs source. That's corrupted input data. The model and version are unchanged. So data_corruption is the best fit. The evidence directly shows values altered (×100). I'll go with data_corruption.\n\nNow, evidence IDs to cite:\n- E0007: compare_tables source vs inputs — monthly_charges ×100 (478 changed, median ratio 100, mean 71.91→7191.06)\n- E0008: evaluate_predictions — log_loss 25.23, accuracy 0.288, roc_auc 0.504, confusion matrix showing 483/500 predicted positive\n- E0009: compare_distributions — no drift vs reference (all q~1)\n- E0003: model_catalog — same model version for reference and active (no version change)\n- E0005: predictions — model_version single value same hash, probability mean 0.9676\n\nLet me pick the key ones: E0007 (corruption), E0008 (performance confirmation), E0009 (no drift), E0003 (same version). I'll cite E0007, E0008, E0009, E0003. Maybe E0005 too. Keep it focused: E0007, E0008, E0009, E0003.\n\nresume: concise, measurement-based.\n\nLet me write the final JSON in French.\n\ncause_probable: data_corruption\nresume: \"L'alerte log_loss (25.23 vs référence 0.507) est expliquée par une corruption des données d'entrée : monthly_charges a été multiplié par 100 entre la source et les inputs reçus par le modèle (478 valeurs modifiées, ratio médian ×100, moyenne 71.91 → 7191.06), tandis que toutes les autres colonnes sont inchangées. Le modèle servi est inchangé (même version de référence et active), la population source ne présente aucun drift par rapport à la référence (tous les tests non significatifs), et le modèle prédit le churn pour 96,6 % des clients (483/500) avec des probabilités extrêmes (moyenne 0,968), d'où le log_loss élevé et un ROC_AUC ~0,50. Cause : corruption des données d'entrée, pas de drift, ni de régression de version du modèle.\"\npreuves: [\"E0007\",\"E0008\",\"E0009\",\"E0003\"]\n\nLet me finalize.",
